Subject: [PATCH 06951/11362] [outer-loop] Task 13: real-reinduction-path A/B
 reverses exp5598's finding (REQ-ARC-WMTE-5599)

Prompted by the operator's cost/benefit question. Investigating E3AgentPolicy._induce_and_plan()
(the method the SCORED live agent actually calls) found its LLM tier is only invoked after a
genuine level-up ("level_up_reinduction") -- for the initial exploration stall, the agent uses a
zero-LLM TTT-prior model plus classical DSL/active-probe tiers instead, confirmed empirically (a
real lb.run_game call on m0r0 completed its internal induce step in 17.6s, far too fast for real
LLM inference). This means exp5596/5597/5598's induction-quality measurements never exercised
the real live-agent reinduction code path -- their roster (no game has ever leveled up)
structurally cannot trigger it.

Built exp5599 to call execute_bounded_llm_reinduction (the exact function the scored agent
invokes) directly on real, reproducible post-level-up transitions from lp85 (the one game this
session confirmed levels up), with a widened n_ctx=22000 fixing lp85's known context overflow at
the default 16384, and 3 stochastic repeats per arm (current vs candidate_27b).

Result reverses exp5598's finding: current plans successfully 1/3 of the time (mean reinduce
duration ~55s); candidate_27b plans successfully 0/3 of the time and takes ~7x longer per
attempt (~401s mean). Disclosed methodology gap: this call used the function's bare-default
gating rather than exactly replicating the real caller's stricter configured values
(min_heldout_accuracy=1.0 plus other policy-specific kwargs) -- current's one "planned" draw
likely would not survive that stricter threshold either. The plan-rate flip alone (n=3) isn't
independently decisive, but the ~7x speed regression is sample-size-independent and severe.

Updated cost/benefit conclusion: do not switch the frozen live-submission generator.
exp5598's induction-quality signal did not carry over to the code path that actually matters for
live play, and the speed-cost risk flagged in the cost/benefit discussion is now empirically
confirmed as large. The frozen live-submission generator remains unchanged.

